PASS  [volume]  a fresh named volume: Docker gives it the image's owner, and the daemon boots  (running, phase: ready, /data is 1000:1000 755 )
PASS  [volume]  a named volume first written by root: refused, naming the uid and the owner  (exit 1: chad serve: cannot write /data. chad runs as uid 1000, gid 1000 and /data is owned by uid 0, gid 0, mode 755. Give the volume to uid 1000, for example: chown -R 1000:1000 <volume> )
PASS  [volume]  a host directory owned by uid 1000: the daemon boots  (running, phase: ready, /data is 1000:1000 775 )
PASS  [volume]  a host directory Docker created for the mount: refused, because Docker makes it as root  (exit 1: chad serve: cannot write /data. chad runs as uid 1000, gid 1000 and /data is owned by uid 0, gid 0, mode 755. Give the volume to uid 1000, for example: chown -R 1000:1000 <volume> )
PASS  [volume]  a host directory owned by uid 1001: refused  (exit 1: chad serve: cannot write /data. chad runs as uid 1000, gid 1000 and /data is owned by uid 1001, gid 1001, mode 755. Give the volume to uid 1000, for example: chown -R 1000:1000 <volume> )
PASS  [volume]  a writable volume whose repo/ belongs to another uid: refused, naming both  (exit 1: chad serve: /data/repo is owned by uid 0, gid 0, mode 755 and chad runs as uid 1000. git refuses a repository owned by another user. Give it to uid 1000: chown -R 1000:1000 /data/repo )
PASS  [volume]  and git does refuse such a repository, which is why the check exists  (fatal: detected dubious ownership in repository at '/data/repo' To add an exception for this directory, call: )
PASS  [volume]  run as another uid on a fresh volume: refused, naming that uid  (exit 1: chad serve: cannot write /data. chad runs as uid 2000, gid 2000 and /data is owned by uid 1000, gid 1000, mode 755. Give the volume to uid 2000, for example: chown -R 2000:2000 <volume> )
NOTE  [volume]  a refused boot leaves nothing on the volume  (0 entries in the uid 1001 directory )
